---

<Base
  title="About — Muhammet Sıddık Bel"
  description="Engineer of two crafts. Mechanical by training, software by obsession. Senior test automation specialist in Istanbul, building the Bluebird fleet at night."
>
  <Header current="about" />

  <!-- ───── Hero ───── -->
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="section-eyebrow">about</p>
      <h1 class="page-title">Engineer of two crafts.</h1>
      <p class="page-sub">
        Born 1991 in Diyarbakır, raised on <em>Back to the Future</em> and a
        DOS prompt, trained as a mechanical engineer, working as a senior
        test automation specialist, building an AI suite called
        <em>Alcyone</em> after hours. Multi-disciplinary by accident,
        durable by intent.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ───── Backstory ───── -->
  <section class="bio-section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="section-eyebrow">backstory</p>
      <div class="bio-text">
        <h3 class="bio-h">1998 — the bird arrives</h3>
        <p>
          I was seven when I watched <em>Back to the Future</em>. The image
          stuck and never left: a craft you could build, an instrument that
          could think, a path you could earn. The image had a name decades
          later — the <em>Bluebird</em>, a vessel; <em>Alcyone</em>, the AI
          aboard, after the brightest star in the Pleiades. <em>Halcyon</em>
          is the English word for a kingfisher (a blue bird) and also for a
          calm and peaceful time. The same image, three names, all blue.
        </p>

        <h3 class="bio-h">1999 — Atari, then waiting</h3>
        <p>
          We had an Atari clone — NES-class hardware that arrived in our
          part of the world about a decade after it was current. Most of
          the technology I grew up around was like this: cycles late, but
          arriving. You learned patience and you learned to build with what
          you had.
        </p>

        <h3 class="bio-h">2001 — DOS, the first frontier</h3>
        <p>
          We finally got a computer. There was no Windows on it. For the
          first months I lived in DOS — <code>dir</code>, <code>format</code>,
          <code>cd</code>, <code>mkdir</code>, <code>cp</code>,
          <code>rm</code>. Typing commands into a black screen and watching
          something happen.
        </p>
        <p>
          I had been wanting to be a scientist. Sitting in front of that
          DOS prompt I came to a different conclusion: physical-world
          science was old territory by then, but the digital frontier was
          empty. <em>This</em> was the new world; here you could still
          discover. From that day forward I wanted to be a computer
          engineer. Windows 98 came soon after, then XP in 2002. My father
          bought <em>Chip</em> magazine every month; I worked through the
          tutorials by following the screenshots, copying the steps even
          when I didn't yet read the words.
        </p>

        <h3 class="bio-h">High school — first game, first stage</h3>
        <p>
          Tenth grade: I built my first game in <em>GameMaker</em> and
          submitted it to TÜBİTAK's national project competition. Around
          the same time I started doing theatre — what would become eight
          years of stage work spanning high school and university.
          Engineering teaches you to think under load; theatre teaches you
          to deliver under pressure. They turn out to be the same
          discipline in different costumes.
        </p>

        <h3 class="bio-h">2017 — teaching myself to ship</h3>
        <p>
          I bought <em>msbel.com</em> early. The first repo on
          <a href="https://github.com/msbel5" class="subtle" target="_blank" rel="noopener">github.com/msbel5</a>
          is from September 2017 — <em>FirstMVCBlog</em>, an ASP.NET MVC
          blog engine. On October 27, 2017, I pushed nine repos in a
          single day. <em>HtmlJavascriptExample</em> is one of them: a
          single-page HTML site driven by jQuery + AJAX, no framework, no
          build step. The README from the time reads <em>"expandable so
          data can be fetched with ajax methods. Simple solution for
          serverless static pages."</em> Nine years later, this site
          you're reading is the same idea — just with more discipline.
        </p>

        <h3 class="bio-h">2018 — KOExamApp, the first thing I deployed</h3>
        <p>
          A job-interview project that I shipped to
          <em>koexamapp.msbel.com</em>. Teachers could build quizzes from
          articles automatically fetched via the <em>Wired.com</em> RSS
          feed; students took the exam on a single page, and after
          submitting, jQuery would dynamically colour the correct and
          incorrect answers without a full reload. A single-page app
          before SPAs were a buzzword. BLL / DAL / UI architecture, rich
          text editor, real auth. The <em>msbel.com</em> domain has been
          serving subdomains since I was 26.
        </p>

        <h3 class="bio-h">2019 — Mahzenler ve Evrenler</h3>
        <p>
          The most literal Turkish translation of <em>Dungeons &
          Dragons</em> you can write. <em>MvE</em> — "dungeons and
          universes" — was my first serious RPG attempt: a multi-project
          C# solution, naive in retrospect, the seed of every RPG I have
          since tried to build. (Six years later they made the D&D movie.
          The opportunity for a faithful Turkish-language D&D project
          shipped in 2019 and I had no channel to ship it widely. Lesson
          logged.)
        </p>

        <h3 class="bio-h">2010 — into mechanical, by accident</h3>
        <p>
          I missed the cutoff for computer engineering on the first
          university entrance exam. Repeated the year, missed it again,
          and ended up in mechanical engineering at <em>Istanbul
          University</em>. I told myself I'd pick up software on the
          side — CAD, control programs, drawing tools, whatever the
          curriculum allowed.
        </p>
        <p>
          The bachelor's took six years instead of four. The thesis was
          on <em>position control of linear motors</em> under
          <em>Yrd.Doç.Dr. Yener Taşkın</em> — and despite the department,
          it was not just paperwork. <em>LabVIEW</em> driving a
          <em>National Instruments PCI-6289 DAQ card</em>; an
          <em>Arduino</em> generating dual- and triple-channel PWM
          signals; a stepper-style position-control loop wrapping the
          linear motor. NI Turkey's distributor (Enovas) actually visited
          the lab during the project. The .vi files — <em>lineermotorpwmv4
          arduino üçlü sinyal.vi</em>, <em>lineermotorpwmv5.2 pci 6289.vi</em>,
          <em>stepper konum kontrolü.vi</em> — still sit in my Drive,
          dated February 2017. I left the department fluent in load paths,
          tolerances, control loops, and the slow physics-first way of
          thinking that has stayed with me since.
        </p>

        <h3 class="bio-h">2018-now — the master's, and an ML thesis on the desk</h3>
        <p>
          The mechatronics master's at <em>Marmara University</em> made
          software inevitable. Coursework done, thesis written, defense
          still pending — manuscript on the desk, date not yet. The
          thesis I'm still proud of:
          <strong>"Predictive Maintenance for Electrical Motors by Using
          Machine Learning."</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          The premise was honest: take an existing paper on motor failure
          prediction and replicate it with new data and a wider model
          bench. An <em>MPU6050</em> gyroscope strapped to a CPU fan,
          weights stuck on the blades to simulate failure profiles, an
          <em>Arduino Mega 2560</em> capturing vibration coordinates. R
          for the first signal pass, Python for the rest. The early ANN
          baseline produced an RMSE of 308; I worked through 35 candidate
          models, GridSearch-optimized the winner, and brought the RMSE
          down to 9.7 time-windows — about 7 seconds. With proper PLC
          sensors that window collapses to milliseconds. The conference
          draft was titled <em>"Estimating Failure Times for Electric
          Motors by Machine Learning."</em>
        </p>
        <p>
          The thesis proposal was first submitted October 27, 2020. Code
          and dataset were ready by July 2021. The proposal was revised
          again in August 2023 with new references. The supervisor signed,
          the manuscript is in good shape, the defense itself still has
          not been scheduled. (One of the three or four open loops in my
          life that I keep meaning to close.)
        </p>
        <p>
          A second project that year went the other direction:
          <em>image-processing-based fault detection on assembly
          lines</em> — defect classification from camera frames, a problem
          I would revisit five years later in a very different shape (a QA
          agent that watches the page).
        </p>

        <h3 class="bio-h">2018-2023 — Huawei</h3>
        <p>
          Five years at <em>Huawei Technologies</em> on telecom-grade
          systems. Performance testing under load. Cross-platform
          regression. The lesson: when the system is large and old and
          honest, the test framework has to be larger and older and more
          honest. I came out of it knowing how to ship at scale.
        </p>

        <h3 class="bio-h">2023-10 — CodeBERT, and "I'm so sorry"</h3>
        <p>
          I built <em>CodeGlimmer</em> in October 2023 — a Java app that
          generated code documentation by combining OpenAI GPT-3 with
          HuggingFace CodeBERT. Around the same time I was running a
          local CodeBERT-tokenized GPT-2 chatbot in IntelliJ; without
          fine-tuning, it would generate the phrase <em>"I'm so sorry!
          I'm so sorry! I'm so sorry!"</em> on an infinite loop. Someone
          screenshotted it; the screenshot turned into an Instagram story
          captioned <em>"why is the first thing an AI says always im so
          sorry?"</em> It is one of my favourite artefacts from the
          early-LLM era — a chatbot, fed someone else's tokenizer, with
          no training, apologising forever.
        </p>

        <h3 class="bio-h">2024-01 — dAIlog, agentic before agentic</h3>
        <p>
          In January 2024 I built <em>dAIlog</em>: a C# / Python hybrid,
          an ASP.NET API in front of a Python AutoGen service running
          <em>seven agents</em> — Coder, Data_analyst, Engineer,
          Scientist, Planner, Critic, Executor — coordinated by a
          ModifiedUserProxyAgent in a GroupChat. A single user prompt
          would trigger Planner → Engineer (writes code) → Scientist
          (no code, classifies and reads) → Critic (doublechecks claims
          and source URLs) → Executor (runs the code) → loop until
          termination. It ran on gpt-3.5-turbo and gpt-4. It worked.
        </p>
        <p>
          I posted a story on January 25, 2024:
          <em>"hikayeler olmasa, gpt 3.5'a yeni basmışken, Claude code,
          openclaw yokken, AI'lar kod analizi yapıyor, agentslar henüz
          makaleyken, 2023'te benim tek cümle ile kod yazıp çalıştıran
          framework yazdığımı inandıramam."</em> ("If it weren't for
          stories — back when GPT-3.5 was new, no Claude Code, no
          OpenClaw, when AIs were doing code analysis and 'agents' was
          still just papers — I couldn't convince anyone in 2023 I'd
          written a framework that runs code from a single sentence.")
        </p>
        <p>
          I proposed dAIlog as an internal product. It went nowhere.
          Six months later <em>"agentic"</em> was the word everyone was
          using. Devin launched. Cursor exploded. Claude Code shipped.
          I had a working version of the same primitive sitting in a
          private repo from January. I kept the code. I kept the lesson.
        </p>

        <h3 class="bio-h">2023-now — Testinium, and building at night</h3>
        <p>
          At <em>Testinium</em> I designed a Python end-to-end framework
          from scratch — Gauge-style DSL, Postman-like API surface, fully
          parallel. It runs Saudi fintech tests on production. The
          private automation work in this period —
          <em>a101ikwebautomation</em>, <em>OrderAutomation</em>,
          <em>ozlukWebAutomation</em>, <em>hadiAutomation</em>,
          <em>comisAutomation</em>, the Iyzico nopCommerce plugin — did
          more to sharpen my instincts than any greenfield project. The
          discipline of writing automation that has to actually run,
          every day, against real e-commerce sites, is a different kind
          of teacher.
        </p>

        <p>
          The fleet now: <a href="/projects/alcyone" class="subtle">Alcyone</a>
          is a 9-layer scientific trading bot on a Pi 5, running 24/7.
          <a href="/projects/pegasus" class="subtle">Pegasus</a> is the AI
          QA agent — it wrote a production-ready Selenium spec for the
          Pegasus Airlines booking flow in two hours, end to end.
          <a href="/projects/ember" class="subtle">Ember</a> is the
          counter-experiment: an RPG written by hand in Unity, because
          the AI-generated version proved unplayable. The lesson there is
          worth its own essay — and probably will be.
        </p>

        <h3 class="bio-h">2026 — Estonia, and a flag worth flying</h3>
        <p>
          E-Residency to Estonia is in flight. €150 paid, card pickup in
          Ankara, 4-8 weeks. Once it lands, an OÜ follows — a one-person
          umbrella for the fleet. Tallinn on paper, Istanbul in the
          captain's chair. I am thirty-five and I am late on nothing that
          matters.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ───── Experience ───── -->
  <section class="experience-section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="section-eyebrow">experience</p>
      <div class="experience-list">
        {experience.map((job) => (
          <div class="job">
            <div class="job-meta mono">
              <span class="job-period">{job.period}</span>
            </div>
            <div class="job-body">
              <h3 class="job-role">{job.role}</h3>
              <p class="job-company">{job.company}</p>
              <ul class="job-notes">
                {job.notes.map((note) => <li>{note}</li>)}
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ───── Education ───── -->
  <section class="education-section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="section-eyebrow">education</p>
      <div class="education-list">
        {education.map((edu) => (
          <div class="edu-item">
            <div class="edu-meta mono">
              <span>{edu.period}</span>
            </div>
            <div class="edu-body">
              <h3>{edu.degree}</h3>
              <p class="edu-school">{edu.school}</p>
              {edu.thesis && (
                <p class="edu-thesis-title"><em>{edu.thesis}</em></p>
              )}
              {edu.thesisDetail && (
                <p class="edu-thesis-detail">{edu.thesisDetail}</p>
              )}
            </div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ───── Stack ───── -->
  <section class="stack-section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="section-eyebrow">stack</p>
      <h2 class="stack-heading">Tools the fleet runs on.</h2>
      <div class="stack-grid">
        {Object.entries(stack).map(([category, items]) => (
          <div class="stack-group">
            <p class="stack-category mono">{category}</p>
            <ul class="stack-items">
              {items.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
            </ul>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ───── Asides ───── -->
  <section class="asides-section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="section-eyebrow">also</p>
      <h2 class="aside-heading">Things that don't fit on a CV.</h2>
      <ul class="asides">
        <li>
          <span class="aside-mono mono">2010-2018</span>
          <span>Eight years of theatre — high school + university stages. Same discipline as engineering, just different costumes.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="aside-mono mono">2017-09</span>
          <span>First repo on GitHub: <em>FirstMVCBlog</em>. Followed by nine repos in a single day on October 27, 2017. <em>msbel.com</em> was already mine and serving subdomains.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="aside-mono mono">2018</span>
          <span><em>KOExamApp</em> deployed on <em>koexamapp.msbel.com</em> — an interview project. Single-page jQuery / AJAX exam app pulling articles from the Wired.com RSS feed. SPAs before the term was a buzzword.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="aside-mono mono">2019</span>
          <span><em>Mahzenler ve Evrenler</em> — a literal Turkish translation of <em>Dungeons & Dragons</em>, written as a multi-project C# solution. Six years before the D&D movie.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="aside-mono mono">2023-10</span>
          <span>The "I'm so sorry!" infinite loop — a CodeBERT-tokenized GPT-2 chatbot, untrained, apologising forever. Screenshot survived as someone else's Instagram story.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="aside-mono mono">2024-01</span>
          <span><em>dAIlog</em> — multi-agent AutoGen framework with seven agents (Coder, Data_analyst, Engineer, Scientist, Planner, Critic, Executor). Built when most engineers still treated LLMs as autocomplete. Proposed as a product. Passed on. I kept the code.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="aside-mono mono">2026-04</span>
          <span><em>ExampleBank Test Automation</em> — last week's private QA repo. AI Snapshot + Locator/Test Autogen flow with auto-generated Step Catalog. The direct ancestor of Pegasus.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="aside-mono mono">42 repos</span>
          <span><a href="https://github.com/msbel5" class="subtle" target="_blank" rel="noopener">github.com/msbel5</a> — a chronological archive going back to 2017. Some are graveyards, some are foundations. None were deleted.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="aside-mono mono">17,180 h</span>
          <span>Lifetime gaming hours per the GOG Galaxy export — about 715 days. Heaviest libraries: Pathfinder (Kingmaker + Wrath, ~680h combined), the three Baldur's Gates (~671h), Fallout 4 / 76 (~1115h), UnderRail (~445h), Witcher 3 (~283h), RimWorld (~286h), Civ V (~280h), Pillars of Eternity, Divinity: Original Sin I+II, Avowed, Outer Worlds 2, Kingdom Come: Deliverance. The reading list of the RPG I am trying to build.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="aside-mono mono">native</span>
          <span>Turkish (TR) — fluent English (EN) — comfortable in both technical and literary registers.</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

